WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.864
Effective detections focus on meaningful behaviors rather than isolated indicators.

00:00:03.864 --> 00:00:07.628
A single indicator may be useful in the right context,

00:00:07.628 --> 00:00:12.144
but durable detections usually look for activity patterns that matter to defenders.

00:00:12.144 --> 00:00:15.531
Detection engineers think carefully about telemetry sources, field availability,

00:00:15.531 --> 00:00:18.919
business context, investigation workflows, analyst usability, and operational impact.

00:00:18.919 --> 00:00:23.435
The best detection logic still fails if the data is unavailable or

00:00:23.435 --> 00:00:26.070
the alert is too vague to investigate.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:28.328
Good detections answer a defensive question.

00:00:28.328 --> 00:00:31.716
They explain what was observed, why it may matter,

00:00:31.716 --> 00:00:35.480
which system or identity is involved, what supporting context exists,

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:38.491
and what the next investigative step should be.

00:00:38.491 --> 00:00:41.125
Signal quality matters more than alert volume.

00:00:41.125 --> 00:00:45.642
A detection program that produces thousands of low-confidence alerts can make the

00:00:45.642 --> 00:00:47.524
SOC slower and less confident.

00:00:47.524 --> 00:00:51.287
A smaller set of well-designed detections can create more value.

00:00:51.287 --> 00:00:56.180
Building effective detections is therefore a design problem, not just a query-writing task.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:58.439
Engineers balance sensitivity, specificity, context, maintainability,

00:00:58.439 --> 00:01:01.826
and operational usefulness so defenders can make informed decisions.

00:01:01.826 --> 00:01:03.331
Context is especially important.

00:01:03.331 --> 00:01:07.472
Useful alerts often include related identity information, asset importance, recent activity,

00:01:07.472 --> 00:01:11.988
known ownership, enrichment results, and links to investigation guidance or case workflows.

00:01:11.988 --> 00:01:14.623
Effective detection design also considers failure modes.

00:01:14.623 --> 00:01:18.763
Engineers ask what could create noise, what data delays may occur,

00:01:18.763 --> 00:01:20.645
what fields may be missing,

00:01:20.645 --> 00:01:24.409
and how the detection should behave when the environment changes.

00:01:24.409 --> 00:01:28.549
Before deployment, detection engineers should also think about the analyst experience.

00:01:28.549 --> 00:01:32.313
A good alert should be understandable under pressure, during handoffs,

00:01:32.313 --> 00:01:35.700
and by someone who did not write the detection.
